<p>If that was your intent, the connection (&quot;devolving into terrified ape-men when confronted with the &#8230; annihilation&quot;) wasn't clear. I upvoted, but that was mostly because I hadn't been expecting an AWCY and was surprised when I got to the end because in retrospect I totally should have seen it coming.</p>
<p>I also have some fairly grave suspension-of-disbelief issues w/r/t skull-restructuring (it seems highly likely to me that &quot;painful&quot; would be the least of one's problems, considering how harmful intracranial pressure can be in less extreme circumstances). I mean, if somebody wanted to vote against it for that alone, I wouldn't hold it against them.</p>
<p>There <em>is</em> some knee-jerk downvoting here at the SCPF (and occasionally some regular <em>jerk</em>-jerk downvoting), but you probably shouldn't mention it when it's your own article under discussion. Being disappointed that an article wasn't better-received is totally understandable and maybe even expected; accusing everyone of bad-faith downvoting because the article wasn't better-received is probably mistaken. Even if it isn't mistaken, saying so 1) makes you look pouty and entitled and 2) isn't going to change any votes.</p>

							</div>

								
					
